.TH TERMCAP 3X "May 15, 1985"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
tgetent, tgetnum, tgetflag, tgetstr, tgoto, tputs \- terminal independent operation routines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <term.h>
.PP
.B int tgetent(char *bp, char *name);
.PP
.B int tgetnum(char *id);
.PP
.B int tgetflag(char *id);
.PP
.B char *tgetstr(char *id, char **area);
.PP
.B char *tgoto(char *cm, int destcol, int destline);
.PP
.B void tputs(char *cp, int affcnt, int (*outc)(int));
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions extract and use capabilities from the terminal capability data
base
.IR termcap (5).
These are low level routines;
see
.IR curses (3X)
for a higher level package.
.PP
.I Tgetent
extracts the entry for terminal
.I name
into the buffer at
.I bp.
.I Bp
should be a character buffer of size
1024 and must be retained through all subsequent calls
to
.I tgetnum,
.I tgetflag,
and
.I tgetstr.
.I Tgetent
returns \-1 if it cannot open the
.I termcap
file, 0 if the terminal name given does not have an entry,
and 1 if all goes well.
It will look in the environment for a TERMCAP variable.
If found, and the value does not begin with a slash,
and one of the names in the TERMCAP string's own first field is
.B name,
the TERMCAP string is used instead of reading the termcap file.
If it does begin with a slash, the string is used as a path name rather than
.I /etc/termcap.
.PP
.B "Note:"
this implementation does
.I not
consult TERM to decide whether the TERMCAP string applies \(em it matches
.B name
against the entry's names, as above.  A TERMCAP entry that does not match falls
through to
.I /etc/termcap
like any other miss.
This can speed up entry into programs that call
.IR tgetent ,
as well as to help debug new terminal descriptions
or to make one for your terminal if you can't write the file
.I /etc/termcap.
.PP
.I Tgetnum
gets the numeric value of capability
.I id,
returning \-1 if is not given for the terminal.
.I Tgetflag
returns 1 if the specified capability is present in
the terminal's entry, 0 if it is not.
.I Tgetstr
returns the string value of the capability
.I id,
places it in the buffer at
.I area,
and advances the
.I area
pointer.
It decodes the abbreviations for this field described in
.IR termcap (5),
except for cursor addressing and padding information.
.I Tgetstr
returns NULL if the capability was not found.
.PP
.I Tgoto
returns a cursor addressing string decoded from
.I cm
to go to column
.I destcol
in line
.I destline.
The result is a static buffer, overwritten by the next call.
.PP
.B "Note:"
4.2BSD's
.I tgoto
consulted the external variables
.B UP
and
.B BC
to avoid placing \fB\en\fR, \fB^D\fR or \fB^@\fR in the returned string.
Neither variable exists here and neither is consulted: the vestige of that logic in
the source appends an always-empty string.  A \fBcm\fR whose substitution would
produce one of those three characters produces it.
.PP
(Programs which call tgoto should be sure to turn off the XTABS bit(s),
since
.I tgoto
may now output a tab.
Note that programs using termcap should in general turn off XTABS
anyway since some terminals use control I for other functions,
such as nondestructive space.)
If a \fB%\fR sequence is given which is not understood, then
.I tgoto
returns \*(lqOOPS\*(rq.
.PP
.I Tputs
decodes the leading padding information of the string
.IR cp ;
.I affcnt
gives the number of lines affected by the operation, or 1 if this is
not applicable,
.I outc
is a routine which is called with each character in turn.
.PP
.B "Note:"
this implementation emits
.I no padding.
The delay is still skipped \(em it is part of the capability string, and a
.I tputs
that did not consume the digits would print them \(em but it is only stepped over,
never converted, so
.I affcnt
is accepted and ignored and the external variables
.B ospeed
and
.B PC
of 4.2BSD do not exist and are not consulted.
Nothing on this machine can be overrun: the console is a Consul-254 driven one
character at a time by the kernel's
.I sc
driver, which offers the next character only when the last has printed.
Leaving those two names undefined is also what lets a
.IR curses (3X)
define them itself, as v7's does.
.SH "BESM-6 NOTES"
.I Tgetent
accepts at most four
.B tc=
indirections, where 4.2BSD allowed 32.  Each one costs about 360 words of stack \(em
two 1024-character buffers at six characters to the word, plus two frames \(em against
a user stack of four pages, so 32 would overflow it in silence.  No real database comes
near four.
.PP
The \fB%\fR forms \fB%n\fR, \fB%>\fR, \fB%B\fR and \fB%D\fR, which 4.2BSD compiled
conditionally, are always available.
.PP
See
.I lib/libtermcap/README.md
in the source tree for the port's account, in particular the four
.B "char *"
comparisons it had to delete.
.SH FILES
.ta \w'<prefix>/share/besm6/lib/libtermcap.a  'u
<prefix>/share/besm6/lib/libtermcap.a	\-ltermcap library
.br
/etc/termcap	data base
.DT
.SH SEE ALSO
termcap(5); curses(3X), when it is ported
.SH AUTHOR
William Joy
